<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Round Orange.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar words and basic phrases. Interactive: Identify objects in the immediate environment in response to simple questions.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize simple sentences about food shapes (e.g., "Oranges are round."). Can follow basic instructions involving food shapes (e.g., "Find the round apple."). Can respond to questions about food shapes using words or gestures (e.g., "What shape is the cracker?" "A square.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 40%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 10%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows real fruits (e.g., an orange, a banana, a lemon) and a cracker. The teacher holds up each item and says its name clearly. "Hello everyone! What do you see? Look! This is an orange. This is a cracker." Students listen and point to the items as the teacher names them. The teacher asks, "What shape is the orange?" and models the response, "It's a round orange." using gestures for "round."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key vocabulary and phrases. Holding an orange, the teacher says, "Listen: Rrround orange." exaggerating the /r/ sound. Then, holding a cracker, "Listen: Crracker. Square cracker." exaggerating the /k/ sound. The teacher also demonstrates using body language and TPR for shapes: drawing a circle in the air for "round" and a square for "square." Students listen and imitate the gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and shapes through simple questions requiring yes/no or pointing responses.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a round orange: "Is this a square orange?" (Students shake heads or say "No.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a cracker: "Is the cracker round?" (Students shake heads or say "No."). "What shape is the cracker?" (Students point to a square flashcard or make a square gesture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story or teacher-read narrative with visual flashcards. The narrative focuses on a child's snack time, introducing food items and their shapes. For example, "Leo's Snack Time."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes: Identifying and describing foods by shape (e.g., "The cracker is square."). Describing food: Using simple words to describe the colors and shapes of fruits and other foods, such as "orange" and "cracker."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Listen and Point.</b> The teacher plays/reads the audio story "Leo's Snack Time." As students listen, the teacher holds up flashcards of the foods mentioned (orange, cracker, banana, juice box, pineapple, mango). Students listen and point to the correct flashcard as each food is mentioned. "Leo eats a round orange. He also has a square cracker."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Listen and Circle.</b> The teacher distributes a simple worksheet with pictures of different foods and shapes. Students listen to sentences from the story (e.g., "The mango is oval. The juice box is square.") and circle the food item that matches the shape described. The teacher repeats each sentence twice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Listen and Show.</b> The teacher makes true/false statements about the story's food items and shapes. Students listen and show thumbs up for "true" or thumbs down for "false." E.g., "The banana is round." (Thumbs down). "The pineapple is big." (Thumbs up).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening to identify adjective + noun (e.g., "round apple"), WH-questions (e.g., "What shape is it?"), Present simple (e.g., "Oranges are round."). Vocabulary: pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, juice box, snack, meal, eat, cut, hold, see, mix, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty, orange, black, brown, white, this, that.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Listen and Point):</b> The teacher says a description (e.g., "a round, orange fruit," "a square cracker," "a long, yellow banana"). Students listen and point to the corresponding flashcard or realia item. Teacher ensures clear pronunciation of /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Listen and Choose):</b> The teacher holds up two contrasting flashcards (e.g., a lemon and a mango). The teacher says, "Listen carefully: 'This is a small, round lemon.'" Students listen and choose the correct flashcard. Repeat with other pairs like "a big, green lime" vs. "a big, yellow papaya" or "a healthy snack" vs. "a tasty cracker."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Guided Listening - Shape Sort):</b> The teacher places three shape outlines (circle, square, oval) on the board or floor. The teacher then says the name of a food item and its shape (e.g., "A round orange," "A square cracker," "An oval mango"). Students listen and physically place a picture card of that food item onto the correct shape outline. This activity reinforces phonemic awareness of sounds like /æ/ in "apple" if an apple flashcard is used for a round shape.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Follow the Recipe for Our Plate" (Listening-based activity).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are given a paper plate or a drawing space and various pre-cut paper shapes representing different foods introduced (e.g., round orange, square cracker, oval mango, yellow banana, green lime). The teacher acts as the chef, giving specific instructions. Students listen carefully and place or draw the correct food shape onto their plate according to the verbal instructions. This directly connects to the "Shapes on Our Plates" scenario and "It's a Round Orange." theme.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Procedure:</b></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Teacher says: "Listen carefully! Let's make our snack plate."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Teacher gives the first instruction: "First, take a round orange. Put it in the middle of your plate." (Students listen and place/draw the round orange).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Teacher gives the next instruction: "Now, take a square cracker. Put the cracker next to the orange." (Students listen and place/draw).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Teacher continues with 2-3 more instructions, incorporating colors and sizes: "Find a yellow banana. It is long. Place the banana under the cracker." or "Find a small, green lime. Put it on top of the orange."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to all instructions and complete their 'snack plate.'</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment of learning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Follow the Recipe" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete their "snack plate" by following the teacher's verbal instructions. The teacher walks around, observes individual students, and checks if they correctly identify and place/draw the food shapes as instructed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening Focus):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Followed Instructions:</b> Student accurately placed/drew shapes based on verbal commands (e.g., round orange, square cracker).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identified Shapes and Foods:</b> Student recognized the specified food items and their shapes (e.g., recognized "cracker" as "square").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Recognized Adjectives:</b> Student understood "big," "small," "orange," "yellow," "green" in relation to food items.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer by showing fingers (1=not good, 2=okay, 3=good).</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I listen carefully to find the round fruits and square crackers?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Could I understand 'big' and 'small' foods?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Learner and Teacher Reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new food shapes did I hear about today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was my favorite part about listening to the food story?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, clarity of instructions). Students were very engaged in the "Follow the Recipe" activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled to differentiate "lemon" and "lime" by sound alone. More focused listening drills on similar-sounding words or colors will be beneficial. I need to ensure I explicitly pronounce /æ/ in "apple" when introducing it next.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read short, illustrated sentences using the same vocabulary and shapes to understand food descriptions in written form, reinforcing the concepts introduced through listening today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>